The three verbs, and what a bare invocation does: exit 2, clap's usage error,
which is the same claim `check-config` makes about a config it refuses — the
question could not be asked (`zenwatch/src/exit.rs`).

```console
$ zenwatch --help
Watch N rules and route every genuine state change to M sinks.

Rules are zenctl watchdog's closed zk2 vocabulary — rate-above, rate-below, silent-for, invalid-payload, qos-mismatch, doctor, alert-firing, dropped — plus `instance-gone <SYSTEM/SERVICE>` (`*` in either position: each address the pattern matches is up while an instance token is held, and gone when none is with a complete read behind it, read from the live presence projection — under a wildcard only an address seen held can go, and an exact address is watched from the start; one notification per address, zenctl watchdog's rule firing from the same reading), `liveliness-gone <SEL>` (the dead-man's switch on liveliness tokens) and `alerts <SEL>` (alarms.v1: each alarm the base-relative selector reaches, read in the deployment's namespace, is firing, resolved or unobservable on its own; one notification per alarm). Three states ride every notification, never two: ok / firing / unobservable, and a drop in the observer is unobservable, never ok (tooling guide O6).

Exit codes: 0 clean, 1 the act failed, 2 refused input (`zenwatch/src/exit.rs`).

Usage: zenwatch <COMMAND>

Commands:
  run           Run the notifier: one process, one config, N rules, M sinks — until SIGINT/SIGTERM
  check-config  Parse and validate a config, refusing exactly what `run` would refuse — exit 0 or 2
  test-sink     Send one synthetic notification through one configured sink — exit 0 delivered, 1 failed, 2 unknown sink
  help          Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

```

```console
$ zenwatch run --help
Run the notifier: one process, one config, N rules, M sinks — until SIGINT/SIGTERM

Usage: zenwatch run [OPTIONS] --config <FILE>

Options:
      --config <FILE>     The JSON5 config file [env: ZENWATCH_CONFIG=]
      --context <NAME>    The named connection context to run against (overrides the config's `bus.context`)
      --namespace <NS>    The deployment namespace the resolved reads open their session in (overrides the config's `bus.namespace`) [alias: --base]
      --contracts <PATH>  Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file, a directory of them, or a `.history` root (added to the config's `contracts`)
      --dry-run           Print every notification that would be sent, as one ndjson line per sink, and send nothing
      --once              Evaluate one tick and exit — a smoke run, not a daemon
  -h, --help              Print help

```

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --help
Parse and validate a config, refusing exactly what `run` would refuse — exit 0 or 2

Usage: zenwatch check-config [OPTIONS] --config <FILE>

Options:
      --config <FILE>     The JSON5 config file [env: ZENWATCH_CONFIG=]
      --context <NAME>    The named connection context to run against (overrides the config's `bus.context`)
      --namespace <NS>    The deployment namespace the resolved reads open their session in (overrides the config's `bus.namespace`) [alias: --base]
      --contracts <PATH>  Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file, a directory of them, or a `.history` root (added to the config's `contracts`)
  -h, --help              Print help

```

```console
$ zenwatch test-sink --help
Send one synthetic notification through one configured sink — exit 0 delivered, 1 failed, 2 unknown sink

Usage: zenwatch test-sink --config <FILE> <NAME>

Arguments:
  <NAME>  The sink's name in the config's `sinks` table

Options:
      --config <FILE>  The JSON5 config file [env: ZENWATCH_CONFIG=]
  -h, --help           Print help

```

```console
$ zenwatch
? 2
Notify when something is wrong on a zk2 Zenoh deployment (#388)

Usage: zenwatch <COMMAND>

Commands:
  run           Run the notifier: one process, one config, N rules, M sinks — until SIGINT/SIGTERM
  check-config  Parse and validate a config, refusing exactly what `run` would refuse — exit 0 or 2
  test-sink     Send one synthetic notification through one configured sink — exit 0 delivered, 1 failed, 2 unknown sink
  help          Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help     Print help (see more with '--help')
  -V, --version  Print version

```
